    "summary": "Tight first set, monster The Other One, stellar Dancin'>Wharf Rat segues",
    "blurb": "The first set rolls tight with crisp guitars and solid rhythm, highlighted by a standout 'Cassidy' and the seamless 'Dancin' In The Street → Wharf Rat → Dancin'' sequence. The second set delivers a monster 'The Other One' plus strong outings on 'Wheel' and 'Brown Eyed Women'. Overall the band is locked in with Jerry front‑and‑center.",
    "review": "On October 10, 1976 at Oakland‑Alameda County Stadium the Dead were firing on all cylinders. The opening half of the concert feels like a masterclass in tight, high‑energy jamming – Jerry’s tone sits right up front, his slide‑laden lick during the Dancin' jam is unforgettable, and Phil’s bass locks perfectly with the dual drummers. Highlights from Set 1 include a punchy 'Cassidy', an exuberant 'El Paso', and the famed three‑way segue of 'Dancin' In The Street → Wharf Rat → Dancin'' that keeps the crowd moving. \n\nSet 2 ramps up the intensity with a monster 'The Other One' that showcases the band’s improvisational depth, while 'Wheel', 'Brown Eyed Women' and the second 'Playing In The Band' keep the momentum rolling into a joyous 'Sugar Magnolia' encore. Keith’s keyboards weave nicely through the mix, especially on 'El Paso' and 'Loser', and Mickey and Bill stay in sync with tasteful hi‑hat work, though they sound a touch tired toward the end. The show is captured best by the Clugston soundboard (gd76-10-10.sbd.clugston.3381.sbeok.shnf), which delivers a clear, Jerry‑forward mix that lets every nuance shine.",
